text:
About this lecture
Next year a lab teacher or thesis supervisor will hand you a spreadsheet and ask: “Can you make a figure and tell me whether the treatment did anything?” Nobody will list the steps for you. Today you practise doing the whole job on familiar data, using your notes and the reference pages.
Today you will:

run the complete workflow again: find, check, analyse, explain, reproduce;
repair a broken analysis and explain results in plain words;
investigate an unusually high value without deleting it;
leave with a project, a report and a checklist you can reuse.

This is the last day of the course. No new method: only practice with what you already know.

Frame the day as rehearsal. Using the reference pages is part of competent practice, not cheating. Students can start at the station where they feel weakest.

About this lecture
In a later lab course, or in your thesis project, you will be handed a table from an experiment and asked: “what did the treatment do?” You will be expected to answer with a short report: the question, a plot, the numbers, one limitation, and code that someone else can run again. Today you do that whole job once, on one small study.
Today you will:

choose one study: duckweed biomass or organic product yield;
check the data and decide what one independent unit is;
plot, summarise and compare in a stated direction;
write a result paragraph and keep it all in one report that renders from scratch.

No new methods today: everything comes from weeks 2–4. Day 24 tests whether someone else can reproduce your report.

Keep the briefing short and leave the time for the work. Students may choose the organic route if their teacher selects it; both routes use only familiar steps. Ask them to keep a note of any step that fails rather than hiding it.

About this lecture
A colleague sends you a single-cell RNA-seq table: 30,000 genes measured in 100,000 cells. You type read_csv() and your laptop freezes, or R stops with “cannot allocate vector”. Before you load a dataset, how can you tell whether it will fit?
Today you will:

learn what the CPU, RAM, disk and GPU each do, and where R keeps its objects;
estimate the RAM a table needs: rows × columns × 8 bytes;
check estimates in R with file.size() and object.size();
size up DNA, images and single-cell data.

Weeks 1–4 used small tables. From now on data size matters: tomorrow (Day 22) we move to a bigger, remote computer.

Keep the opening concrete: the single-cell example returns at the end of the lecture with the arithmetic. Students do not need to buy or build computers; they need a rough working model so that error messages and slow computers make sense, and so they can decide when a laptop is not enough. All sizes in this lecture were measured in R on the course data or computed from the stated numbers.

About this lecture
In a biochemistry lab you put your samples in a spectrophotometer or plate reader. The instrument does not report “mg/L”: it reports an absorbance, a number without units. To turn it into a concentration you first measure standards of known concentration, fit a line through them, and read your sample’s concentration backwards from that line. What concentration is in my sample, and when can I trust the number?
Today you will:

fit a calibration line with lm() and read its intercept and slope, with units;
check the residuals: how far each standard lies from the line;
convert an unknown sample’s absorbance into a concentration;
refuse to report a reading outside the standards, and use a dilution instead.

Days 17–18 compared means. Today and on Day 20 we fit straight lines; today the line is a measuring tool.

These are simulated readings for a fictional colour assay; no chemistry course is needed. Ask whether anyone has already used a standard curve (protein assays such as Bradford, DNA quantification, enzyme activity). Every one of those is today’s workflow.

About this lecture
You send your supervisor a figure and one line: “treated plants grew more.” The replies come back: Which data file? How many plants? What are the dots? Did any values go missing? Can I rerun it? Every one of those questions is fair, and a short report can answer all of them at once. How do we write a report that a reader can understand, check, and regenerate?
Today you will:

give each part of a short report a job: question, data and methods, results, interpretation;
name the input file and the checks, so every number has a visible origin;
render from a fresh session at the project root, so the report rebuilds itself from saved files;
keep claims at the level of the evidence: describe the sample, without claiming a tested effect.

This day pulls together Days 11–14. The same report becomes the starting point for the comparisons in week 4.

The point is not formatting or length. A good descriptive report is short, specific, and reproducible. The practical has a core seedling report (descriptive-report.Rmd) and an organic-yield alternative (organic-report.Rmd); students produce one, chosen by the teacher.

About this lecture
A thesis draft shows a bar chart with two bars: mean growth of control and treated seedlings. The examiner asks: how many plants are behind each bar? How much did they vary? Is one bar pulled up by a single unusual plant? The chart cannot answer, because the plants have disappeared into two numbers. How do we summarise groups and still let the reader see the plants?
Today you will:

make a summary table with one row per group, including how many plants contributed;
plot individual plants with a mean marker, and define any whiskers you draw;
use a histogram to see a distribution, and notice how much the bin width shapes it;
save the table and the figure as files, with a caption a reader can check.

This builds on Day 12’s mean and SD; the table and figure go straight into the descriptive report on Day 15.

Many published figures still show bars of means only. Journals increasingly ask for individual data points for small samples, exactly because of the examiner’s questions above. Today’s practical file is day13-groups.R.

About this lecture
A labmate exports the seedling measurements from the lab spreadsheet and sends you the file. You calculate the group means and email them to your supervisor. A week later someone notices that one plant appears twice and one length was typed in centimetres. R never complained: it calculates a precise-looking answer from whatever table it is given. How do you know the table is fit to calculate from?
Today you will:

inspect a raw file as text before turning it into numbers;
repair only what source evidence supports, in a script, keeping the original file unchanged;
keep an unknown value unknown, and count plant records and usable measurements separately;
save and re-check the cleaned file so that someone else can trace every change.

Week 3 is about describing data. The checked table and these habits carry into Days 12–15 and into the comparisons of week 4.

Motivate from the student’s own future: every lab spreadsheet, field sheet and instrument export contains slips. The skill is not suspicion of every unusual value; it is knowing which changes evidence supports and leaving a trail. The practical file data/seedlings-messy.csv has planted problems, and data/field-notes.txt contains the source evidence.

About this lecture
You have a script, a figure and a few numbers. The usual next step is to paste the figure into Word and type the numbers into sentences. Then one value in the data is corrected, and the document keeps the old numbers. Months later, nobody, including you, can say which version of the data produced Figure 2. An R Markdown report keeps the explanation, the code and its results in one file, and rebuilds all of them from the data each time you render it.
Today you will:

read the parts of an .Rmd file: header, text and code chunks;
render it into an HTML report you can open in any browser;
put a calculated number into a sentence, so it updates itself;
find and fix the first error when rendering stops.

Day 10 uses this to write your first complete short report. Every report in this course is made this way.

Lab reports, thesis chapters and supplementary methods all suffer from copied numbers that no longer match the data. The course webpages are made with Quarto, a close relative; students only need R Markdown.

About this lecture
Your supervisor sends a CSV file with lengths of 24 seedlings, and later a results file with many thousands of rows. Remember Day 1: opening a table in a spreadsheet and saving it again can quietly change the data. How do we bring a table into R unchanged, and check that it contains what we think it contains?
Today you will:

make sure R is looking in the right folder for the file;
import a CSV file into a table and inspect its size, columns and types;
tell labels from measurements, and a missing value from a zero;
write down what you checked, so someone else can trust the table.

Day 6 typed values by hand. From today the values come from files. Day 8 calculates and plots from the imported table.

Day 1 showed gene names such as SEPT2 turned into dates; only call back to it here. The new example is real: in October 2020 Public Health England missed about 16,000 positive COVID-19 results because results were passed through the old .xls format, which holds at most 65,536 rows. Neither is a reason to fear spreadsheets for looking at data; they are reasons not to let a program silently rewrite your only copy.

About this lecture
You now have 24 seedlings in a file. Did the treated ones grow more? A spreadsheet can answer that once. But you will measure again next season, and your supervisor will ask how you got the number. From next week, R does the calculations and keeps the instructions, so the answer can be made again.
Today you will:

open the course project in RStudio and find your way around its window;
tell the R Console apart from the terminal;
run a first calculation and a supplied plot;
watch a finished report being made from saved files.

This is an orientation day. Day 6 explains R step by step.

Keep it light. Students do not need to understand the code today; they need to know where to type and where to look. The motivation is the same as on day 1: results should come from saved files that you can find and use again. If someone has not installed R/RStudio yet, they follow along and use the setup page before day 6.

About this lecture
Why type commands when you can click? Because the computers that hold large biological data, such as a sequencing facility’s server or a university compute cluster, often have no desktop and no mouse. You log in and type. And a command you have written once can be repeated on 200 files instead of 200 rounds of clicking.
Today you will:

open a terminal and read its prompt;
ask where you are (pwd) and what is there (ls);
create folders (mkdir) and move between them (cd);
recover calmly when a command fails.

The folders are the same ones you clicked through on Days 1 and 2. We use these commands again on Day 4 and on the remote computer in week 5.

Keep the motivation concrete: big data lives on remote Linux machines, and those are driven by typed commands. Do not promise that the terminal is faster for everything; for one file, clicking is fine. Windows students use Ubuntu, Mac students use Terminal, as on the setup page.

About this lecture
Did a treatment make seedlings grow more? The measurements are in a file. Some kinds of files any program can read; others only one program understands, and some programs change your data without asking. Which kind of file should hold our measurements?
Today you will:

download the course folder;
open different files in a plain-text editor and see what is inside;
compare the same table as CSV and as Excel;
save a plain-text note.

No programming today. Every later day reads and writes plain-text files.

Students know roughly what a file is; today is about what is inside different kinds of files. Keep it concrete: open files, look, compare. The seedling data are simulated teaching data.

The difference in means
pull() takes one column out of a table as a plain vector of numbers. Use it to make one vector per group, then find out how much more the treated plants grew:

treated &lt;- seedlings |&gt;
  filter(treatment == "treated") |&gt;
  pull(growth_mm)
control &lt;- seedlings |&gt;
  filter(treatment == "control") |&gt;
  pull(growth_mm)

observed_difference &lt;- mean(treated) - mean(control)
observed_difference

The result is 6.22 mm: treated plants grew on average 6.22 mm more than controls. A difference is a number with a unit and a direction, so always write it as “6.22 mm (treated − control)”. The sign comes from the order of the subtraction: mean(control) - mean(treated) gives −6.22 mm for the same plants.

Repairing a copy in code

cleaned &lt;- raw |&gt;
  mutate(treatment = tolower(trimws(treatment)),
         initial_mm = if_else(plant_id == "P18", "18.7", initial_mm),
         final_mm = if_else(plant_id == "P21", "43.8", final_mm)) |&gt;
  distinct() |&gt;
  mutate(initial_mm = parse_double(initial_mm),
         final_mm = parse_double(final_mm),
         growth_mm = final_mm - initial_mm)

Each line carries out one sentence of the field notes. trimws() removes the spaces around a value and tolower() turns Control into control. if_else(condition, yes, no) replaces a value only in the row of the named plant. distinct() removes rows that are identical in every column, which here is the second P01. parse_double() then turns the checked text into numbers, and P06’s empty final length becomes NA, R’s code for a missing value. The CSV file is never edited, so anyone can rerun the script and see every repair.

Another lab: percentage yield
Use this instead of the seedling report if your teacher chooses it. Twelve independent synthesis runs in data/organic-recovery.csv were randomly assigned to a standard or a modified protocol, six each. The question is: what percentage of the possible product was collected in each run, and how does that differ between protocols? Calculate yield_percent = 100 * isolated_mass_g / theoretical_mass_g (Y01 gives 62%) and summarise by protocol:

organic &lt;- read_csv("data/organic-recovery.csv", show_col_types = FALSE) |&gt;
  mutate(yield_percent = 100 * isolated_mass_g / theoretical_mass_g)
organic_summary &lt;- organic |&gt;
  group_by(protocol) |&gt;
  summarise(n_runs = n(), mean_yield_percent = mean(yield_percent),
            sd_percentage_points = sd(yield_percent), .groups = "drop")
organic_summary

Follow the same four parts, with checks of run IDs, protocol labels, missing values and positive theoretical masses. You should find mean yields of 68.67% and 77.00%, with SDs of 4.32 and 3.74 percentage points (standard and modified). Plot one point per run with the y-axis “Isolated product yield (%)”. In the limitation, say that the product is assumed pure, so yield says nothing about purity. Render with "organic-report.Rmd" in the command above.

Station 4: your complete report
Open your report from day 23 (duckweed-report.Rmd or organic-study.Rmd). Restart R and render it without running anything in the Console first. Then read the HTML as a stranger would, and find out whether it tells you:

the question and the independent unit (a flask, or a synthesis run);
the input file, the units and what happened to missing values;
a plot of individual units and a small summary;
the comparison in a stated direction, with size, spread or uncertainty, and a limitation.

Fix whatever is missing and render again. The organic study stays descriptive: group means and SDs are enough.

About this course
Biology is now measured in files: plate-reader exports, microscope images, sequencing reads, field records. In every lab course, in your thesis, and in most jobs after it, someone will hand you data and ask what it shows.
In five weeks you will learn to:

organise and inspect data files, and use a terminal;
analyse a table in R instead of clicking in a spreadsheet;
describe variation and compare groups honestly;
write a report that anyone can re-run and get the same numbers.

You do not need any programming experience.

Set the tone: this is a practical course for biologists, not a programming course. Everything is anchored in biological measurements, mostly a simulated seedling experiment. The next three slides answer the question students will have: why not just use Excel, which they already know?

About this lecture
R will not click through folders for you. To read the seedling measurements it needs a written address: how do you write down exactly where a file is, so that it works on every computer?
Today you will:

draw the course project as a tree of folders;
write absolute and relative paths;
see why a relative path needs a starting folder;
check that saved work is on disk.

Tomorrow the terminal uses these paths; from week 2, R does.

Start by asking students to find yesterday’s note, day01-notes.txt, through the folders, not through “recent files”. The folders they opened, in order, are the path. Course data are simulated.

About this lecture
A lab group sends you lab-data.zip: four seedling pots, two treated and two controls, one length measurement each. Which samples were treated, and are they longer? Data often arrive like this, as a ZIP of several files. Before any statistics you need to see what you received and pick out the right records.
Today you will:

list and unpack a ZIP in the terminal;
read the manifest that says which file belongs to which sample;
pick out lines with grep, count them with a pipe, and save them with &gt;;
work on a copy, never on the original.

This is the last shell day. On Day 5 you meet R.

The archive is a small, separate practice dataset (four pots), not the 24-plant dataset students will use in R. Keep the question in view all session: which pots are treated, and how long are they? Every command today answers part of that.

About this lecture
A seedling was 20 mm long at the start and 37 mm at the end. A calculator gives 17 mm. But a real experiment has dozens of plants, a value gets corrected, and your supervisor asks you to redo it. If the calculation is written down, you run it again. If it was done by clicking, you start over and hope you remember every step.
Today you will:

calculate in the R Console;
store results under names;
keep several plants’ values together and summarise them;
save a script that rebuilds everything after R restarts.

This is the first day of writing R. From Day 7 the numbers come from a file instead of being typed.

Start from the biological question, not from an overview of programming languages. All example plants are simulated. The point of the day: a saved, rerunnable list of steps. Students met RStudio’s panes on Day 5.

About this lecture
The seedling table is in R. The question is: how much did each plant grow, and did treated plants grow more? In a spreadsheet you would drag a formula down a new column and click a chart together. When a value is corrected next week, you would repeat every step by hand. Here, the new column and the figure come from a few lines of code that you simply run again.
Today you will:

add a calculated column (growth) with mutate();
keep the rows you want with filter() and the columns you want with select();
draw a labelled figure with ggplot(), where each point is one plant;
save the figure and the table as files other programs can open.

This builds on Day 7’s import. On Day 9 the code and the figure go into one report.

The biological question drives the day: growth per plant and a first look at the groups. No statistics today; we describe what we see. The organic-yield and dilution tables in the exercises use the same steps on lab calculations.

About this lecture
A lab report, a thesis chapter or a paper is read by someone who was not in the room and will not run your code first. They need to know what was measured, how, what you saw, and what you cannot conclude. A report that announces “the treatment works!” from two overlapping clouds of points, or that only renders on your own laptop, fails that reader. Today you write a short report that answers a biological question honestly and that someone else can rerun.
Today you will:

build a report from four short sections: question and data, methods, results, interpretation;
explain a method in biological words, with the code underneath;
describe a figure honestly: pattern, overlap and one limitation;
check that the project renders from a fresh session and from another folder.

This day brings together Days 6–9. Day 11 asks what to do when the table itself contains errors.

This is a consolidation day: no new R functions. The skill is putting existing pieces together for a reader. Keep the bar realistic: four short, correct sections beat a long formal document.

About this lecture
In a plant physiology lab you measure the photosynthesis rate of ten plants, then raise the light and measure the same ten plants again. The plants differ a lot from each other before anything changes. Did the rate go up, and by how much? The same design appears whenever a patient, an animal or a culture is measured before and after something: blood pressure before and after exercise, a leaf before and after shading.
Today you will:

keep the two readings of each plant together, using its identifier;
turn each pair into one change and look at the ten changes;
run a paired t-test and read its estimate, interval and p-value;
say what a before/after design can and cannot show.

Day 17 compared two separate groups. Today each unit is measured twice, which needs a different analysis. Day 19 moves on to calibration lines.

Open with the design, not the test. Ask the room for other before/after examples from their own biology courses. The aim of the day is that students recognise a paired design when they see one, keep the identity of each unit, and do not report the result as if light alone caused the change.

About this lecture
Plant ecologists often need the dry mass of leaves, but drying and weighing destroys the leaf. Leaf area is easy to measure from a photo or scan. If area and mass go together, a line can describe how. We collected one leaf from each of 18 plants and measured both. Do larger leaves have more dry mass, and how much more per cm²?
Today you will:

plot two measured variables and fit a line with the familiar lm();
state the slope with units: “about 4.11 mg per cm²”;
use residuals and a sensitivity check without deleting data;
keep apart association and cause, and stay inside the observed range.

Day 19 used a line as a measuring tool for prepared standards. Today the same code describes a biological pattern, and the interpretation changes. Week 5 turns to the computer itself.

Ask for other examples where one measurement stands in for another: body length and mass in fish, tree diameter and biomass, wing length and age. In each case it matters whether the relation is a description of a sample or a cause. The data are simulated.

Plan the copy before you make it


Write reports/transfer-plan.txt first:

source computer and project path;
destination computer and project path;
the input file (data/algae.csv) and the checks to repeat;
the output you expect back.





Source and destination copies name the algae file and the content checks to repeat on each side.




For the local rehearsal, use two different parent folders, both labelled local. Paths are not secrets, but passwords and login codes never belong in these files. A plan written afterwards can hide a wrong assumption.

About this lecture
Your duckweed report is finished. Your supervisor wants to check it, a lab partner wants to add next month’s flasks, and you will need it again for your thesis. If someone else opens your project, do they get the same report? Often they do not: the report quietly relied on something that only existed on your screen.
Today you will:

render the report from a fresh R session, so it cannot lean on hidden objects;
pack the project as a ZIP and test it as the receiver, in a new folder;
handle corrections: a ZIP is a snapshot, and it is not a backup;
separate “it renders” from “it is right”.

Day 23 produced the report; today makes it shareable. Day 25 consolidates the whole workflow.

Ask who has ever received a file that “doesn’t work” or a spreadsheet whose numbers nobody could re-derive. That is today’s problem. The methods are not new: restart, Knit, zip, unzip. The new thing is doing them as a deliberate test from the receiver’s side.

The problem: it works on my screen


A report can run for you and fail for anyone else because it depends on:

an object you created in the Console, not in the report;
a package loaded only in the Console;
a personal path such as C:/Users/anna/Desktop/duckweed.csv;
a data file that is not in the project.

You will not notice any of these on your own computer.




An object imported in the interactive Console is not part of the saved report, so the report cannot find it in a fresh session.




All four are invisible as long as you stay in the same session on the same laptop. That is why the tests today change the session (restart) and the location (new folder).
